WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.166
You know Maya Angelou
was a mentor for me,

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:04.375
and when she was invited
to write the inaugural poem,

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:07.792
as I shared with you,
for President Clinton,

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.166
I sent her the coat and gloves

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.667
and she wore them
so beautifully.

00:00:11.708 --> 00:00:13.375
And I wanted to continue
that tradition,

00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:16.792
but you'd already chosen
your fabulous coat...

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.375
- Amanda: Yes.
- ...that's now gone viral

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.583
along with your red headband.

00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:22.583
So I sent you the ring,
a caged bird,

00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:26.375
paying tribute to Maya,
for that seminal book of hers.

00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:31.792
Do you feel like the baton
of those who came before you

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:35.166
has been passed down
to and through you?

00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:37.792
Oh, that's a great question,
and it meant so much to me

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:41.625
to have my own piece of Maya
with me in that moment

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:42.959
because as I mentioned,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.375
when I told myself
every time I wrote a poem

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:48.166
to write a poem worthy
of an inauguration,

00:00:48.208 --> 00:00:49.959
I would listen to Maya's poem.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.792
I would listen to
"On The Pulse Of Morning"

00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:56.333
probably every single morning
of my last year of college.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:00:59.792
So it was great to kind of
exist in that same space

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:02.375
in the inauguration.
And when I think--

00:01:02.417 --> 00:01:04.166
I'll stop you right there.
Let me stop you right there.

00:01:04.208 --> 00:01:06.500
Why were you listening
to her poetry

00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:08.166
from the inauguration?

00:01:08.208 --> 00:01:09.792
Why were you listening
to "On The Pulse Of Morning,"

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.583
which was her inaugural poem?

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:13.166
Why were you listening
to that every morning

00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:15.166
your last year of college?

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.959
I would listen
to Maya's poem so often

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.583
just because for me
I was in a deep place

00:01:21.625 --> 00:01:27.166
where I was trying to
analyze the power of poetry

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:29.583
to speak for a people
and speak for a populace,

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:31.166
and to do so with hope.

00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:34.125
And having Maya's poem
as an example

00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:36.166
not only of written word

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:40.250
but also incredible spoken word
that she brought to it,

00:01:40.291 --> 00:01:44.166
I wanted to continue
to participate

00:01:44.208 --> 00:01:46.583
in that tradition
of hope-making

00:01:46.625 --> 00:01:48.375
in every single poem
that I wrote.

00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:51.166
So that's how I grounded
myself every morning.

00:01:51.208 --> 00:01:54.000
And I've read that you
so related to Maya's story

00:01:54.041 --> 00:01:59.166
also growing up because
of the loss of her voice

00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:03.542
and your being able
to incorporate

00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:05.125
what she was able to do

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:07.959
with you really retraining
yourself how to speak.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.875
When I first heard that,
I went, "Well, whoa.

00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:14.041
That is just a miracle."

00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:17.291
It was an amazing discovery
when I was reading

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:18.458
"I Know Why
The Caged Bird Sings,"

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.000
her autobiography.

00:02:20.041 --> 00:02:22.583
And reading about
her issues with speech,

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:25.792
I almost was kind of angry
with every single educator

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:28.000
that I'd encountered
up to that point.

00:02:28.041 --> 00:02:30.583
I was like, I'm a black girl
with a speech impediment,

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:32.959
and no one thought
it was relevant to tell me

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.583
that this great orator
that I'm reading

00:02:35.625 --> 00:02:37.417
had a similar struggle?

00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:41.166
So being able to connect
with her and relate with that

00:02:41.208 --> 00:02:44.375
was a real beacon for me
in my life.

00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:47.417
It's so difficult to be
that which you cannot see,

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:51.000
to dream that which
not has become itself yet,

00:02:51.041 --> 00:02:53.333
and so I really looked to her
for inspiration.

